1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,305
Imagine standing near the top of the world. The

2
00:00:03,305 --> 00:00:06,329
sky above you is so dark it's almost black.

3
00:00:06,329 --> 00:00:10,267
Every step takes several breaths. Your fingers are numb,

4
00:00:10,267 --> 00:00:13,642
your thoughts are slow, and your body is quietly

5
00:00:13,642 --> 00:00:17,158
dying. Welcome to the death zone of Mount Everest.

6
00:00:17,158 --> 00:00:20,463
By the end of this video, you'll understand why

7
00:00:20,463 --> 00:00:23,557
it's one of the deadliest places a human can

8
00:00:23,557 --> 00:00:23,979
stand.

9
00:00:24,529 --> 00:00:27,993
Welcome to Earth Decoded. If you love the most

10
00:00:27,993 --> 00:00:32,286
extreme places on our planet, tap subscribe. Today, we're

11
00:00:32,286 --> 00:00:34,470
climbing into the death zone.

12
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:39,632
Mount Everest rises eight thousand eight hundred forty-nine meters

13
00:00:39,632 --> 00:00:42,986
above sea level, on the border between Nepal and

14
00:00:42,986 --> 00:00:46,480
China, in the Himalayas. It's the highest point on

15
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:48,716
Earth's surface above the ocean.

16
00:00:49,266 --> 00:00:53,724
In Nepal it's called Sagarmatha, and in Tibet, Chomolungma,

17
00:00:53,724 --> 00:00:57,351
often translated as Goddess Mother of the World.

18
00:00:57,901 --> 00:01:01,895
The Himalayas exist because India is crashing into Asia.

19
00:01:01,895 --> 00:01:05,818
That collision began about fifty million years ago, and

20
00:01:05,818 --> 00:01:09,384
it's still pushing Everest up by a few millimeters

21
00:01:09,384 --> 00:01:10,168
every year.

22
00:01:10,718 --> 00:01:14,650
Near the summit, climbers walk over yellowish rock full

23
00:01:14,650 --> 00:01:17,796
of tiny fossils of sea creatures. The top of

24
00:01:17,796 --> 00:01:20,870
the world was once the bottom of an ancient

25
00:01:20,870 --> 00:01:21,299
ocean.

26
00:01:21,849 --> 00:01:26,618
On May twenty-ninth, 1953, Edmund Hillary from New Zealand

27
00:01:26,618 --> 00:01:30,812
and Tenzing Norgay, a Sherpa from Nepal, became the

28
00:01:30,812 --> 00:01:34,265
first people known to stand on the summit.

29
00:01:34,815 --> 00:01:38,741
But maybe they weren't the first. In 1924, British

30
00:01:38,741 --> 00:01:43,137
climbers George Mallory and Andrew Irvine were last seen

31
00:01:43,137 --> 00:01:46,827
high on the mountain, heading for the top. They

32
00:01:46,827 --> 00:01:50,753
never came back. Mallory's body was found in 1999,

33
00:01:50,753 --> 00:01:54,207
but nobody knows if they reached the summit.

34
00:01:54,757 --> 00:01:58,138
Since then, thousands of people have reached the top.

35
00:01:58,138 --> 00:02:01,136
But more than three hundred people have died on

36
00:02:01,136 --> 00:02:04,198
the mountain, and many of their bodies are still

37
00:02:04,198 --> 00:02:07,579
up there, because bringing them down is so dangerous.

38
00:02:08,129 --> 00:02:11,427
Quick question. Would you ever try to climb Everest?

39
00:02:11,427 --> 00:02:12,950
Tell me in the comments.

40
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:16,874
The journey starts at Everest Base Camp in Nepal,

41
00:02:16,874 --> 00:02:20,937
at about five thousand three hundred meters. That's already

42
00:02:20,937 --> 00:02:24,794
higher than any mountain in the lower forty-eight United

43
00:02:24,794 --> 00:02:25,276
States.

44
00:02:25,826 --> 00:02:29,295
Climbers spend six to nine weeks on the mountain.

45
00:02:29,295 --> 00:02:32,338
They go up and down between camps again and

46
00:02:32,338 --> 00:02:35,595
again, so their bodies can slowly adapt to the

47
00:02:35,595 --> 00:02:38,285
thin air. It's called acclimatization.

48
00:02:38,835 --> 00:02:42,207
Right above base camp is the Khumbu Icefall, a

49
00:02:42,207 --> 00:02:45,799
frozen river of ice cracked into giant blocks and

50
00:02:45,799 --> 00:02:49,977
deep crevasses. It moves constantly. Climbers cross it on

51
00:02:49,977 --> 00:02:52,616
aluminum ladders laid over the gaps.

52
00:02:53,166 --> 00:02:56,892
The ground itself can betray you. In April 2015,

53
00:02:56,892 --> 00:03:01,083
a huge earthquake in Nepal triggered an avalanche that

54
00:03:01,083 --> 00:03:05,508
swept through Everest Base Camp, killing more than twenty

55
00:03:05,508 --> 00:03:06,051
people.

56
00:03:06,601 --> 00:03:09,965
It's one of the most dangerous parts of the

57
00:03:09,965 --> 00:03:14,267
climb. In 2014, an avalanche here killed sixteen Nepali

58
00:03:14,267 --> 00:03:17,161
mountain workers in a single morning.

59
00:03:17,711 --> 00:03:20,783
Then comes the Lhotse Face, a wall of blue

60
00:03:20,783 --> 00:03:24,294
ice more than a kilometer high, climbed on fixed

61
00:03:24,294 --> 00:03:28,317
ropes. And after that, at around eight thousand meters,

62
00:03:28,317 --> 00:03:30,511
climbers enter the death zone.

63
00:03:31,061 --> 00:03:33,765
Why is it called the death zone? At the

64
00:03:33,765 --> 00:03:37,163
summit of Everest, the air pressure is only about

65
00:03:37,163 --> 00:03:39,728
one third of what it is at sea level.

66
00:03:39,728 --> 00:03:42,779
Every breath you take has only about a third

67
00:03:42,779 --> 00:03:45,205
of the oxygen your body is used to.

68
00:03:45,755 --> 00:03:49,684
Above eight thousand meters, the human body can't adapt

69
00:03:49,684 --> 00:03:53,327
anymore. Even resting, it starts to break down. You

70
00:03:53,327 --> 00:03:56,899
lose weight, you can't sleep well, and your organs

71
00:03:56,899 --> 00:03:58,470
are starved of oxygen.

72
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:02,156
If someone from sea level were suddenly dropped at

73
00:04:02,156 --> 00:04:05,606
the summit without extra oxygen, they would likely lose

74
00:04:05,606 --> 00:04:07,425
consciousness within minutes.

75
00:04:07,975 --> 00:04:10,823
The thin air can cause fluid to build up

76
00:04:10,823 --> 00:04:14,169
in the lungs, called HAPE, making you feel like

77
00:04:14,169 --> 00:04:17,870
you're drowning. Or fluid in the brain, called HACE,

78
00:04:17,870 --> 00:04:21,501
causing confusion, stumbling and strange decisions.

79
00:04:22,051 --> 00:04:25,571
That's one of the scariest parts. Your judgment fails

80
00:04:25,571 --> 00:04:28,627
right when you need it most. Climbers have sat

81
00:04:28,627 --> 00:04:30,819
down to rest and never gotten up.

82
00:04:31,369 --> 00:04:35,316
Add the cold. Near the summit, temperatures are often

83
00:04:35,316 --> 00:04:39,710
below minus twenty-five degrees Celsius, and winds from the

84
00:04:39,710 --> 00:04:43,360
jet stream can blow faster than one hundred sixty

85
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:47,530
kilometers per hour. Frostbite can take fingers and toes

86
00:04:47,530 --> 00:04:48,201
in hours.

87
00:04:48,751 --> 00:04:52,588
Your body burns enormous energy just to stay warm

88
00:04:52,588 --> 00:04:56,346
and keep moving, but at that altitude you barely

89
00:04:56,346 --> 00:05:00,184
feel hungry. Many climbers lose ten kilos or more

90
00:05:00,184 --> 00:05:01,828
during an expedition.

91
00:05:02,378 --> 00:05:05,635
And if something goes wrong up there, help is

92
00:05:05,635 --> 00:05:09,254
far away. Helicopters struggle to fly in such thin

93
00:05:09,254 --> 00:05:13,090
air, so rescues above about seven thousand meters are

94
00:05:13,090 --> 00:05:16,709
extremely rare. In the death zone, you mostly have

95
00:05:16,709 --> 00:05:18,229
to get yourself down.

96
00:05:18,779 --> 00:05:21,453
If you could ask one question to someone who

97
00:05:21,453 --> 00:05:24,067
stood on the summit, what would it be? Tell

98
00:05:24,067 --> 00:05:25,221
me in the comments.

99
00:05:25,771 --> 00:05:29,919
So how does anyone survive? Most climbers breathe bottled

100
00:05:29,919 --> 00:05:33,994
oxygen through a mask above about seven thousand meters.

101
00:05:33,994 --> 00:05:36,978
It doesn't make it easy. It just makes it

102
00:05:36,978 --> 00:05:37,633
possible.

103
00:05:38,183 --> 00:05:41,636
A few have done it without. In 1978, Reinhold

104
00:05:41,636 --> 00:05:45,551
Messner and Peter Habeler became the first to reach

105
00:05:45,551 --> 00:05:50,386
the summit without extra oxygen, something many doctors thought

106
00:05:50,386 --> 00:05:51,537
was impossible.

107
00:05:52,087 --> 00:05:56,358
Summit day usually starts around midnight. Climbers move slowly

108
00:05:56,358 --> 00:05:59,950
through the dark, with headlamps, hoping to reach the

109
00:05:59,950 --> 00:06:02,797
top in the morning and get down before the

110
00:06:02,797 --> 00:06:06,457
afternoon winds. They set a strict turnaround time: if

111
00:06:06,457 --> 00:06:09,236
they're not on top by then, they go down,

112
00:06:09,236 --> 00:06:11,202
no matter how close they are.

113
00:06:11,752 --> 00:06:14,792
Because most of the dying happens on the way

114
00:06:14,792 --> 00:06:18,454
down, when climbers are exhausted and their oxygen is

115
00:06:18,454 --> 00:06:19,283
running out.

116
00:06:19,833 --> 00:06:24,235
Nothing on Everest happens without the Sherpa people. Born

117
00:06:24,235 --> 00:06:27,879
and raised at high altitude, they fix the ropes,

118
00:06:27,879 --> 00:06:31,902
carry the loads and guide most expeditions. Kami Rita

119
00:06:31,902 --> 00:06:35,849
Sherpa has climbed Everest more than thirty times, a

120
00:06:35,849 --> 00:06:36,835
world record.

121
00:06:37,385 --> 00:06:41,480
And Everest has become crowded. In 2019, a famous

122
00:06:41,480 --> 00:06:45,408
photo showed a long line of climbers waiting on

123
00:06:45,408 --> 00:06:49,504
the summit ridge. Waiting in the death zone burns

124
00:06:49,504 --> 00:06:53,599
oxygen and time. That season, eleven people died.

125
00:06:54,149 --> 00:06:58,343
The crowds also leave trash. Old tents, oxygen bottles

126
00:06:58,343 --> 00:07:01,682
and waste have piled up on the mountain for

127
00:07:01,682 --> 00:07:06,498
decades. Today, climbers must carry rubbish down, and clean-up

128
00:07:06,498 --> 00:07:09,061
campaigns remove tons every year.

129
00:07:09,611 --> 00:07:12,206
For the few who make it, the reward is

130
00:07:12,206 --> 00:07:14,733
a view of the curve of the Earth, and

131
00:07:14,733 --> 00:07:18,079
peaks like Lhotse and Makalu, some of the tallest

132
00:07:18,079 --> 00:07:21,152
mountains in the world, far below their feet.

133
00:07:21,702 --> 00:07:25,344
Climbing it isn't cheap either. A permit from Nepal

134
00:07:25,344 --> 00:07:28,844
now costs fifteen thousand US dollars, and a full

135
00:07:28,844 --> 00:07:32,415
expedition can cost from tens of thousands to well

136
00:07:32,415 --> 00:07:34,843
over one hundred thousand dollars.

137
00:07:35,393 --> 00:07:39,228
So, could you survive the death zone? Without months

138
00:07:39,228 --> 00:07:44,242
of training, acclimatization, bottled oxygen, expert guides and good

139
00:07:44,242 --> 00:07:48,150
weather, almost certainly not. Even with all of that,

140
00:07:48,150 --> 00:07:49,772
nothing is guaranteed.

141
00:07:50,322 --> 00:07:53,934
As climbers like to say: the summit is optional,

142
00:07:53,934 --> 00:07:55,890
getting down is mandatory.

143
00:07:56,440 --> 00:08:00,409
Last question. Which extreme place should we explore next?

144
00:08:00,409 --> 00:08:02,051
Tell me in the comments.

145
00:08:02,601 --> 00:08:06,398
Thanks for watching Earth Decoded. If you enjoyed this,

146
00:08:06,398 --> 00:08:09,574
like the video, subscribe, and share it with a

147
00:08:09,574 --> 00:08:13,095
friend who dreams of climbing mountains. See you in

148
00:08:13,095 --> 00:08:13,993
the next one.

